WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.006
Most teams reach a point where language models are running in five places, on three vendors, with no shared record of what any of it costs.

2
00:00:08.006 --> 00:00:10.540
AxonLLM is a control plane for that problem.

3
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:16.048
AxonLLM configures 51 models across thirteen provider adapters.

4
00:00:16.048 --> 00:00:19.010
Forty-six are production-price-ready with shipped pricing, all behind one OpenAI-compatible wire format.

5
00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:32.019
Every request crosses the same fifteen stages: authentication, injection scanning, PII redaction, then the quota hierarchy of organisation, business unit, project and environment.

6
00:00:32.019 --> 00:00:36.670
The budget check happens before a provider is billed, not after.

7
00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:39.290
Adopting it is a one-line change.

8
00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:46.040
You keep the OpenAI SDK your code already uses and point its base URL at the gateway.

9
00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.740
No new client library, no rewrite.

10
00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:59.040
Behind that one endpoint sit thirteen adapters — Bedrock, Anthropic, OpenAI, Google and the rest — each translated to a common format, including tool calls and streaming.

11
00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:07.750
This is the operator's view of a running gateway: 66 requests, $1.26 of spend, and an 87.5% cache hit rate across two projects.

12
00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:16.940
Ten providers, health-checked continuously, so cost and availability are things you watch, not things you reconstruct from an invoice.

13
00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:27.695
Every call is recorded individually: which model answered, which provider served it, the user it belonged to, tokens, cost and latency — averaging about 1.4 seconds here.

14
00:01:27.695 --> 00:01:32.250
This is the audit trail, and it is one trail for all thirteen providers.

15
00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:34.868
The catalogue is where routing is configured.

16
00:01:34.868 --> 00:01:43.245
Each model carries its own strategy — least-latency, cost-optimised or round-robin — and its own ordered list of providers to fall back through.

17
00:01:43.245 --> 00:01:46.910
Changing how traffic is routed does not touch application code.

18
00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:49.803
And this page is the one worth showing a sceptic.

19
00:01:49.803 --> 00:01:58.068
Five of 55 provider mappings lack verified prices, so production excludes them from routing.

20
00:01:58.068 --> 00:02:02.850
That leaves 46 of 51 configured models production-price-ready, and makes the gap visible.

21
00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:05.973
One API, every model, full control.

22
00:02:05.973 --> 00:02:13.290
AxonLLM is open source under MIT-0, and runs with a single docker compose command.
